  "result": "Tool calling is like ordering food in a restaurant.\n\n**Why it matters:** a language model can only write text. It cannot check your booking calendar by itself. Tool calling is how it gets work done in the real world.\n\n**The analogy:**\n- The model is the customer. It sits at the table and cannot go into the kitchen.\n- The menu is the list of tool descriptions. It tells the customer what they can ask for.\n- The order slip is the tool call. The customer writes \"one calendar check, Friday\". This is only text.\n- The kitchen is your program. It reads the slip, does the work, and sends back the dish.\n- The dish is the tool result. The customer reads it, then orders more or stops.\n\n**Example:** a student asks, \"Is the laser cutter free Friday?\" The model writes a slip. Your program checks the calendar. The answer comes back. The model tells the student.\n\n**Where it breaks down:** a real customer sees the kitchen is open. The model only knows what the menu says. A bad description means a bad order.\n\nQuestion: in the makerspace assistant, who actually checks the calendar: the model or your program?",
